{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel of non-experts is more reliable because the checklist acts as a 'distributed error-correction network' that decomposes a complex gestalt judgment into independent binary verifications, reducing the probability of a single-point cognitive failure.","counterconditions":["The checklist items are logically interdependent rather than independent.","The 'training' of non-experts introduces a shared systemic bias (correlated error).","The task requires a synthesis of data that cannot be decomposed into discrete checklist items."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The checklist transforms the task from a high-dimensional pattern matching problem (intuition) into a series of low-dimensional, independent boolean checks. While the expert's intuition is a single integrated pipeline where one blind spot can invalidate the entire result, the panel's reliability emerges from the statistical improbability of multiple non-experts simultaneously failing the same objective binary check, effectively 'averaging out' individual cognitive noise.","optional_refs":["0a07b5048a67c4213e4e113cc978ea24c78a2e1e847b7b43e5b18a021b1dc074"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["0a07b5048a67c4213e4e113cc978ea24c78a2e1e847b7b43e5b18a021b1dc074","c0fa0e146ae4ca340e7fb5ff6a1a4470c67c0b20c15a624c8bf6e9f46cd3f8da"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The single expert is more reliable because the 'checklist' for non-experts is a lossy compression of the expert's mental model, creating 'blind spots' where the checklist is satisfied but the outcome is wrong.","counterconditions":["The checklist is exhaustive and covers all possible failure modes.","The expert's intuition is prone to 'over-fitting' or confirmation bias on verifiable data."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Expert intuition is not 'guessing' but a high-speed execution of an implicit, high-resolution heuristic. A checklist provided to non-experts is 